      {
        "id": "p23-q8-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac7x-\\dfrac5y=3$ and $\\dfrac2x+\\dfrac{25}{2y}=12$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p23-q8-a-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=1,\\quad y=\\frac54",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p23-q8-a-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use reciprocal variables to form a linear system.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Let $u=\\frac1x$ and $v=\\frac1y$. Then:\n$$\\begin{align*}7u-5v&=3\\\\4u+25v&=24\\end{align*}$$\nFive times the first equation gives $35u-25v=15$. Adding the second:\n$$\\begin{align*}39u&=39\\\\u&=1\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting:\n$$\\begin{align*}7(1)-5v&=3\\\\-5v&=-4\\\\v&=\\frac45\\end{align*}$$\nHence $x=1$ and $y=\\frac54$, both nonzero as required.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 78
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q8-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$9x^2+y^2=52$ and $xy=8$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p23-q8-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "(x,y)=(2,4),\\ (-2,-4),\\ \\left(\\frac43,6\\right)\\text{ or }\\left(-\\frac43,-6\\right)",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p23-q8-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Substitute for one variable and solve the quadratic in its square.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Since $xy=8$, $x\\ne0$ and $y=\\frac8x$. Substituting:\n$$\\begin{align*}9x^2+\\frac{64}{x^2}&=52\\\\9x^4-52x^2+64&=0\\end{align*}$$\nLet $t=x^2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}9t^2-52t+64&=0\\\\(9t-16)(t-4)&=0\\\\t&=\\frac{16}{9}\\text{ or }4\\end{align*}$$\nThus $x=\\pm\\frac43$ or $\\pm2$. From $y=\\frac8x$, the pairs are $(\\frac43,6)$, $(-\\frac43,-6)$, $(2,4)$ and $(-2,-4)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 85
      }
    ]
  },
